WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.442
Now let's create a simple grouped summary.

00:00:02.442 --> 00:00:08.082
The goal is one row per diagnosis, with a count of admissions inside each diagnosis.

00:00:08.082 --> 00:00:14.162
The diagnosis column defines the groups, and COUNT calculates a measure within each group.

00:00:14.162 --> 00:00:19.297
Before running the query, predict which diagnosis will have the largest count.

00:00:19.297 --> 00:00:22.437
Then run it in the browser and inspect the result.

00:00:22.437 --> 00:00:27.627
Notice that the original admission rows have been collapsed into a smaller summary table.

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:33.087
We no longer see individual admission IDs because the output grain is diagnosis.

00:00:33.087 --> 00:00:39.202
If you copy this query into pgAdmin, try adding ORDER BY admission count descending.

00:00:39.202 --> 00:00:41.577
Sorting does not change the grouping.

00:00:41.577 --> 00:00:45.407
It simply makes the largest groups easier to identify.

00:00:45.407 --> 00:00:50.747
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:50.747 --> 00:00:57.152
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:57.152 --> 00:00:59.467
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:04.992
In a real setting such as a retail report comparing revenue across products and regions,

00:01:04.992 --> 00:01:11.432
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:01:11.432 --> 00:01:12.987
before discussing syntax.

00:01:12.987 --> 00:01:19.514
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
